{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And Aaron shall come into the Tent of Meeting.</b> Our Rabbis said that this is not the place of this verse, and they gave the reason for their words in tractate Yoma (Yoma 32). They said: the entire passage was spoken in order, except for this entry, which is after the offering of his burnt-offering and the burnt-offering of the people and the burning of the emurim (portions) of the bull and the goat that are performed outside in golden garments, and he immerses and sanctifies his hands and feet and strips them off and dons the white garments, and comes into the Tent of Meeting to bring out the censer-pan (כף) and the firepan (מחתה) in which he burned the incense in the innermost sanctum, and strips off the linen garments after he has brought them out and dons golden garments for the afternoon daily offering — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 16:23). And in truth this verse tells us to interpret it, for it is not possible in any way that He would command Aaron to come into the Tent of Meeting for nothing, only to strip off his garments there and to stand naked in the sanctuary of HaShem and to leave them there to rot. Rather, of necessity, \"And Aaron shall come into the Tent of Meeting\" — to perform a service that the verse did not need to mention, and that is the removal of the כף and the מחתה. And the reason of the verse is that at the outset it mentioned, \"And he shall put the incense upon the fire before HaShem, and the cloud of the incense shall cover the mercy-seat that is upon the testimony, and he shall not die\" (Leviticus 16:13), to say that he should place the incense upon the fire until the cloud of incense rises and he departs, and he leaves the כף and the מחתה there, and in any case he needs to bring them out. Therefore it says, \"And Aaron shall come into the Tent of Meeting,\" to come from inside the curtain for that which he left there. Now, the passage did not mention everything the priest does at the outset in golden garments, such as the morning daily offering, but it began with the day's service in white garments, and arranged the passage so that in them he performs the incense of the innermost sanctum and the inner goat and the matter of the scapegoat, and all of this is done in one sequence, and nothing remained to be done in these garments except the removal of the כף and the מחתה. And the way of Scripture in every place is to complete the matter with which it began, even though it involves something later than what it will mention afterwards. Therefore it says, \"And Aaron shall come into the Tent of Meeting\" in these garments, to complete his services in them, and that is the removal of the כף and the מחתה which he needs to bring out from there. And after he goes out, he strips off his garments that he put on in the morning when he came into the holy place, and leaves them there in the place where he strips them off, to teach that he shall not wear them on another Yom Kippur. And so all that is done in white garments throughout the day is completed in one sequence. And it returned and said, \"And he shall wash his flesh in water, etc., and put on his garments\" — the ones known to him, in which he serves all year, to teach that he requires immersion between every change of garments, and it says, \"And he shall go out and make his burnt-offering,\" for everything done before this in white garments is all an inner service, while his ram and the people's ram that he will mention are performed on the outer altar. Now, the passage (Leviticus 16:4) mentioned the first donning of the white garments and required immersion for it, and it mentioned (Leviticus 16:24) the final stripping-off and required immersion for it, and we learn that he requires immersion for every change of garments. And according to Rashi's opinion, this immersion for the removal of the כף and מחתה will be after his ram and the people's ram, between the additional offerings and the afternoon daily offering. And so is the opinion of all the Geonim, and so it appears from the baraita cited in Torat Kohanim (chapter 6, section 5) as its plain sense. But we found in the Jerusalem Talmud (Yoma 7:2), Rabbi Yochanan said: all agree concerning the removal of the כף and the מחתה that it is after the afternoon daily offering. And so wrote the Rav, Rabbi Moshe (Mishneh Torah, Laws of the Yom Kippur Service 2:2), and so is the sequence recorded in our Mishnah (Yoma 7:1): he sanctified his hands and feet and stripped off the garments and went down and immersed, they brought him golden garments, he put them on and sanctified his hands and feet, and entered to burn the incense. Rather, this is the sequence: the sequence of the day in white garments, and his ram and the people's ram and the additional offerings and the emurim of the sin-offering and the afternoon daily offering in golden garments with a third immersion, and the removal of the כף and מחתה in white garments with a fourth immersion, and the burning of the limbs and the libations and the lamps in golden garments with a fifth immersion. And the reason for this is that since it is not a service, they delayed it until after all the day's actions, provided they fulfill five immersions through it. And therefore they interrupted it in the sequence of the afternoon daily offering, and they bring out the כף and מחתה between the daily offering and the incense."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The Mishnah citation יומא ע refers to Yoma 7:1 (chapter 7, mishnah 1), as indicated by the context of the paragraph; the single letter ע is the standard abbreviation for chapter 70 in the standard numbering of tractate daf/mishnah, here pointing to the mishnaic passage beginning at that point."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:54:53.282332312Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}